    <Vertical id="wpj1_Step_9">
          <Markdown>
              Before you go on, click on the button that says "Analyze My Writing Habits"
              and read how the computer describes you as a writer. Think about it. Do
              you agree or disagree with anything the computer said. Was there anything
              you learned about yourself that surprised you?
          </Markdown>
          <ActionButton label="Analyze My Writing Habits">
              <LLMAction target="wpj1_writing_habits_analysis">
                  BACKGROUND INFORMATION:
                   For the survey pages that ask about writing for school,
                   Our column headers are:
                         Daily (corresponding to 0),
                         Weekly (corresponding to 1),
                         Monthly (corresponding to 2),
                         Every Semester (corresponding to 3),
                         Rarely or Never (corresponding to 4)

                   Our row headers are:
                         Fiction (including drama and fan fiction and graphic novels),
                         A list (e.g. to do list or grocery list),
                         An essay,
                         A text message or direct message,
                         An annotated bibliography
                         A group chat or message board (e.g. Discord or Reddit),
                         A podcast or vlog (video blog) post,
                         Notes on a lecture or passage,
                         Poetry or song lyrics,
                         A physical note (short message) or letter
                         An email,
                         A research paper,
                         A chat with an AI chatbot,
                         A blog entry,
                         A post on social media
                         A Powerpoint or other multimedia presentation,
                         A summary or review,
                         A report,
                         A comment or response on social media,
                         Other writing activities not listed here or on previous screens
                   Our data that addresses writing for school includes:
                   <Ref id="wpj_ref9" target="wpj1_writing_frequency_1" />
                   <Ref id="wpj_ref10" target="wpj1_writing_frequency_3" />
                   <Ref id="wpj_ref11" target="wpj1_writing_frequency_5" />
                   <Ref id="wpj_ref12" target="wpj1_writing_frequency_7" />

                   For the survey pages that ask about writing outside of school,
                   Our column headers are:
                         Daily (corresponding to 0),
                         Weekly (corresponding to 1),
                         Monthly (corresponding to 2),
                         Rarely (corresponding to 3),
                         Never (corresponding to 4)

                   Our row headers include:
                         Fiction (including drama and fan fiction and graphic novels),
                         A list (e.g. to do list or grocery list),
                         An essay,
                         A text message or direct message,
                         An annotated bibliography
                         A group chat or message board (e.g. Discord or Reddit),
                         A podcast or vlog (video blog) post,
                         Notes on a lecture or passage,
                         Poetry or song lyrics,
                         A physical note (short message) or letter
                         An email,
                         A research paper,
                         A chat with an AI chatbot,
                         A blog entry,
                         A post on social media
                         A Powerpoint or other multimedia presentation,
                         A summary or review,
                         A report,
                         A comment or response on social media,
                         Other writing activities not listed here or on previous screens

                   Our data that addresses writing outside of school includes:
                   <Ref id="wpj_ref13" target="wpj1_writing_frequency_2" />
                   <Ref id="wpj_ref14" target="wpj1_writing_frequency_4" />
                   <Ref id="wpj_ref15" target="wpj1_writing_frequency_6" />
                   <Ref id="wpj_ref16" target="wpj1_writing_frequency_8" />

                  All of the data can be interpreted by mapping each number onto the column header it corresponds
                  to and using the corresponding row headers to indicate the frequency with which
                  the student writes in each genre.

                  The student's writing can be analyzed along the following dimensions:
                  1. Academic writing tasks for school purposes, including essays, reports,
                      summaries, notes on a lecture or passage, Powerpoints or other multimedia
                      presentations, letters, research papers, and annotated bibliographies,
                      and chatbot conversations.
                  2. Academic writing tasks undertaken outside of school, including essays, reports,
                      summaries, notes on a lecture or passage, Powerpoints or other multimedia
                      presentations, letters, research papers, and annotated bibliographies,
                      and chatbot conversations.
                  3. Creative writing tasks like fiction, poetry or song lyrics, blogs, podcasts or vlogs,
                      and multimedia presentations done outside of school.
                  4. Everyday social media activities, including emails, posts and comments to
                      social media sites when not required for school, text messages/direct messages,
                      blogs, podcasts, and/or vlogs when not required for school.
                  5. Social media activities done for school purposes, including posts and comments to
                      social media sites when not required for school, and text messages/direct messages,
                      blogs, podcasts, and/or vlogs when not required for school.

                  DIRECTIONS:
                    If the student's response data is missing, note that and say nothing more;
                      otherwise, using the student's response data and addressing the student
                    as you, describe succinctly in single-level bullet point form with bolded headings using Markdown
                    how often the student undertakes writing tasks
                    along each of the major dimensions (academic writing for school, academic
                    writing outside school, creative writing, everyday social media activities,
                    and social media activities done for school), providing an interpretation
                    of the student's overall pattern of writing behavior in terms of the
                    student's likely overall writing identity.
               </LLMAction>
           </ActionButton>
           <LLMFeedback id="wpj1_writing_habits_analysis" render="markdown" />
    </Vertical>
